00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
magic leap is a augmented reality  company founded in  florida in about 2010 and  their vision was huge absolutely huge  they wanted to bring some really really  cutting edge technology that was  previously only available in labs in  huge rooms and shrink all that down into  a form factor similar to a pair of  glasses that you could wear  it's really really cool technology and i  want to talk about  why they're in the news what they could  have done differently  and how they ended up where they are so  magic leap was founded by ronnie abavitz  in 2010  and to date they've raised over two  billion dollars from google and a bunch  of other venture capitalists as well as  large technology firms they recently  announced a large  swath of layoffs about 50 of their  workforce that's 1 000  jobs and they also announced that they  would be pivoting away from  consumer plans and instead focusing on  the enterprise  then about a month later in may they  announced that they had successfully  raised 350 million dollars  and that they wouldn't need to lay off  so many people but  then just a few days ago they announced  that the ceo and founder  would be stepping aside and that's  generally not a good sign for the  company  he's the one that laid down the vision  and obviously they're in a rough  situation if he has to move on and  they're bringing in new management so i  think this is an interesting case study  because  they raised so much money pre-product  market fit  but unlike a theranose or you know  another kind of  real buzzy startup that's raised lots of  money before getting customers  the founder here ronnie is the real deal  he founded mako surgical  which is another kind of hard tack  robotics company  they make surgical arms to help with  knee surgeries and hip replacements  and he sold this company for 1.65  billion dollars  just nine years after founding it to  stryker which is a very big medical  devices company  huge success obviously after an exit  like that  he could get in the room with any type  of investor and they'd want to hear him  out and see what his next vision was  ronnie is clearly incredible at  fundraising  there were multiple articles about how  much money he'd raised before  launching anything or really announcing  anything publicly  so when investors look to value a  company they're  hoping to look at profits if profits  aren't there then revenues might suffice  if revenues aren't there then you might  look at customers or  user traction but if the customers  aren't even there yet because you're  just completely pre-launched  really all you have to go off of is the  technology  and the people behind the project so in  magix leaps  example they were very focused on this  technology demo  and the people and the talent that had  joined the team to make this a reality  if you look around at people who had  done the demo  it was a very secretive process you  needed to sign a lot of non-disclosure  agreements but it sounds like you moved  into a large room  and the equipment was not in a glasses  form factor  at all it was maybe the size of a room  or a large table and it worked  incredibly well  everyone who tried it loved it and was  absolutely  taken aback by it and swore by it  but the vision was always to shrink that  technology down  to something much smaller that you could  wear on your face as a pair of glasses  and that was the real challenge and  that's why they raised so much money  to try and make that happen so the demo  promised a bunch of different things  retinal projection meaning the light  would be projected directly onto your  eyes as opposed to  say you looking at a screen they wanted  to have a wide field of view  deep depth of field so there were  various focal points that you could look  at  made it all look very very real  obviously all packed into a glasses form  factor  at a price that a consumer albeit a  high-end one could afford  but again shrinking that tech down was  just super difficult  one of the biggest problems was the  field of view it was very narrow  and as you tilted your head left or  right objects would clip  in and out of the scene so when i first  heard about this  retinal projection idea i thought it was  just  marketing speak i thought that they had  basically  looked at the science behind what  happens when you look at a led tv  which technically the light does come  from the tv directly to your retina  that that is technically accurate and i  thought that they had just rebranded  looking at a screen or bouncing  something off of a piece of glass into  your eye  as retinal projection but it appears  that they actually  had built a true retinal projection  system  they just couldn't shrink it down so  naturally tons of investors who  looked at this demo and felt this  amazing experience  we're immediately comparing it to the  iphone and multi-touch and just kind of  the  exciting new frontier of technology that  could be unlocked by  a device like this but when i think  about  the iphone launch i think back to the  original steve jobs presentation  i focus on the fact that he had three  main value propositions  the iphone was going to be better than  your ipod better than your current phone  and it was also going to serve as an  internet communicator  or web browser and i think augmented  reality  needs a three-prong approach i think  gaming and these interesting  creative demos aren't enough it also  needs to supplant  video it needs to supplant your tv or  your home theater  it needs to supplant your multiple  monitor setup that you might use for  work  at an affordable price point and i think  that once we get to a point where  vr or ar is able to actually  compete with the existing technologies  be they monitors or  tvs then you'll see really mass adoption  and then all of the creators will come  and start taking advantage of  the broader technology and what's  available with a new device  so when you look at what happened with  the iphone they were able to introduce  very quickly  a product that was better than your  existing cell phone it did the job of  making phone calls reliably it did the  job of handling voicemail and it  actually did that  more efficiently with visual voicemail  and of course as the ipod  it evolved that to include multi-touch  and a larger library  and eventually internet connectivity so  there were a lot of ways that it was  advancing existing devices and replacing  them  so to the consumer they weren't just  buying  a phone that one day would have a large  app ecosystem and they could  maybe download instagram if that was  ever even created  instead they were very easily able to  justify the purchase as  this will replace my current phone it  will replace my ipod  and i'll also be able to browse the web  on this now the browsing the web part is  very important  because it allowed them to  wrap a whole host of benefits around an  existing ecosystem which was  the web the safari web browser on ios  was so mature  that it could render the new york times  website just as you saw it on desktop  and that was something that no mobile  phone could really do at the time  so that was a very interesting approach  because it allowed them to  essentially have a whole host of content  available to the product  immediately there was no real waiting  period  so the analogy with vr and ar here in  gaming  might look like a developer kit that  allows  a traditional developer who's developing  for ps4  or xbox or pc to be able to bring their  content  very easily to vr or ar  much like websites were immediately  viable  on the iphone you might need some  translation software you might need some  developer kits but being able to bring a  huge  library of content and titles to the  device would immediately add value  for someone who maybe doesn't have a tv  or doesn't have a ps4 or doesn't have  monitors and a  gaming rig if they can buy this even if  it's a thousand dollars that might  actually be  cheaper for them so i think that that is  something that  these products need to focus on before  they have their quote unquote iphone  moment  so obviously magic leap is in a lot of  trouble it's the ceo's out  they're potentially laying people off  they're pivoting it doesn't seem like  they've been able to shrink down that  initial demo  in an effective way it seems to be  mostly r d at this point  but my hopes for the future of ar are  still really high i really hope that  this  doesn't cause a winter or some sort of  pullback in investment  you know some of these projects can move  into big tech  some of them can move into the  government and some of them can move  into university research labs but i do  think startups  can have an impact in these spaces so i  really hope that this doesn't mean  that we're not going to see another hard  tech company get  funded in the next five years i i really  hope that  people don't see this as a massive  failure for the industry as a whole  i do think that there's a valuable  lesson here though in  building too much hype before you go  live and you have something that  can actually be used by a consumer the  gap between  the demo and the reality was so wide  with magic leap  they just had an impossible hurdle to  jump over it was very very difficult  for them to bring something to market  and when they finally did they were met  with a lot of criticism and a lot of  negative reviews  because they created so much hype that  they weren't able to fulfill  i'm still super excited about virtual  reality and augmented reality  and i think that if they can solve some  of those other use cases like work  or home theater that the adoption will  be so much higher  ultimately it comes down to an economic  equation for most consumers  if this device can actually save them  money and get them a better experience  they're going to buy it  the amount of people that are gonna  spend a thousand dollars two thousand  dollars on a product that  only has a few demos and not a lot of  content and doesn't actually replace any  of the technology that they currently  have  is very small but as soon as you allow  people to replace  something existing with something that's  better even if it's incremental  the adoption is going to be huge so  that's what i'd like to see from magic  leap  i hope that they can pull through this  and wind up shipping some amazing stuff  i'd really like to be using this on a  daily basis but  unfortunately the product's just not  there yet and  it sounds like they've had some really  tough setbacks and their initial vision  didn't really pan out  but i hope that you know maybe they have  to sell off some patents  maybe some other people pick up the  research but i hope it continues because  the the vision here is incredible and i  hope that it pans out  thanks for listening to this if you have  any comments uh please let me know i'd  love to know what you think about magic  leap and  augmented reality and virtual reality  more broadly thanks a lot

